function pz_what_is_cat_mats_removal_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>This guide covers how to safely remove fur mats from a cat's coat, and when a mat is bad enough that it needs a groomer or vet instead of a home fix. Mats are tangled clumps of fur that form when loose hair, dirt, and oils bind together, usually in spots a cat cannot easily reach or groom well, like behind the ears, under the armpits, and around the base of the tail.</p>
    <p>Small, loose mats are a manageable at-home task. Large, tight mats pulled right up against the skin are a different situation: cat skin is thin, and scissors used incorrectly near a mat are one of the most common causes of accidental cuts during home grooming.</p>
    <p>The goal of this guide is to help you tell the difference and handle each kind the right way, rather than reaching straight for scissors on a mat you cannot see the base of clearly.</p>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

function pz_why_important_cat_mats_removal_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>Mats are more than a cosmetic issue — left alone they can cause real pain and skin problems.</p>
    <div class="pz-why-grid">
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">😿</span>
        <div><strong>Mats pull on the skin constantly</strong><p>A tight mat tugs at the skin with every movement, which is uncomfortable at best and can cause bruising or sores underneath at worst.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🦠</span>
        <div><strong>Trapped moisture breeds skin problems</strong><p>Mats trap moisture, dirt, and urine or feces close to the skin, which can lead to irritation, hot spots, or infection underneath where you cannot easily see it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">✂️</span>
        <div><strong>Home scissor removal is a common injury cause</strong><p>Because mats sit flush against loose, thin cat skin, sliding scissors underneath to cut a mat is a frequent way owners accidentally cut their cat, even with good intentions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🩺</span>
        <div><strong>Severe matting can hide underlying illness</strong><p>A cat that suddenly stops grooming and develops widespread mats is sometimes signaling pain, obesity limiting flexibility, or an illness, not just neglecting the coat on its own.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

function pz_steps_cat_mats_removal_guide() {
    return [
        ['title' => 'Assess the mat', 'desc' => 'Check its size, how close it sits to the skin, and whether it moves when you gently tug at the base. Small and loose mats are candidates for home removal; large, tight ones against the skin are not.'],
        ['title' => 'Work in good light with the cat calm', 'desc' => 'Pick a quiet moment, not right after play or a scare, and have good lighting so you can see exactly where the mat ends and skin begins.'],
        ['title' => 'Separate the mat with your fingers first', 'desc' => 'Gently tease the edges apart by hand before reaching for any tool, which loosens smaller mats without needing to cut anything.'],
        ['title' => 'Use a mat-splitting comb or detangling spray on tighter mats', 'desc' => 'A dematting comb with angled teeth, or a little cornstarch worked into the mat, can help separate strands without pulling the skin.'],
        ['title' => 'Always hold a flat comb or your fingers between the mat and the skin if cutting is unavoidable', 'desc' => 'This creates a barrier so scissors cannot reach the skin, and use blunt-tip grooming scissors, not sharp sewing scissors.'],
